// PS256 or EdDSA; RS256 is the default for new instances; keys in a key management service support
// RS256 and ES256). A new key signs from now on, and a secondary key (for APIs set to the other
// algorithm) rotates with it; the previous public key stays in the JWKS for `grace_period_seconds`
// (default 86400, at most 2592000) so issued tokens and relying-party JWKS caches keep verifying.
// A second rotation inside a running grace window is a 409 unless `emergency: true`, which
// publishes ONLY the new key (every token signed by the old one stops verifying at once — for a
// suspected compromise) and discards a staged key. Audited as signing_key.rotated. `staged: true`
// (or the `prepublish_days` policy) instead pre-publishes: the new key goes into the JWKS after
// the current keys and starts signing at `activates_at`, when the old key becomes previous for 24
// hours; listed as `status: next`, cancelled with DELETE /v1/instance/signing_keys/next, audited
// and sent as signing_key.staged, then signing_key.activated.
